  "notes": [
    {
      "id": "note_28",
      "text": "The violin overlay draws a mirrored kernel-density outline behind each box, showing the FULL shape of the distribution where the box shows only five summaries. That matters when a distribution is bimodal: the demo's two outer groups have near-identical boxes (same median, similar spread), but the violin reveals one is a single hump and the other is two — a structure the box cannot show. Jittered raw points confirm what the density is smoothing. Bandwidth defaults to Silverman's rule (adjust it if the violin looks over- or under-smoothed), and hiding the box gives a pure violin plot. Kernel density estimation: Silverman (1986); violin plots: Hintze & Nelson (1998).",
      "x": 40,
      "y": 24,
      "width": 360,
      "height": 190
    }
